{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Why this exists\n\n`irongrey-chubby-oxpecker` (`aa7b-5e47-6d3d-4615`) is the bug: the Jira reconcile bridge's\nPRIMARY comment-dedup skip — `binding_store.is_comment_mapped(local_comment_key)`, keyed on\ncomment ID — was **inert in production**, because the `comment_ids` map in\n`.bridge_state/bindings.json` is empty:\n\n```\nbindings:     2475 entries\ncomment_ids:     0 entries\n```\n\nThis ticket is not that bug. It is the question the bug raises: **how did a silently-inert\nprimary guard survive review, tests, CI and months of production, and what would have caught\nit?**\n\n## Why it is worth asking here specifically\n\nThree properties make this a high-value escape to study, not a routine one:\n\n1. **It was silent by construction.** `_record_comment_id`\n   (`dispatch_apply_phases.py:309-334`) returns quietly on any of four guard conditions. Nothing\n   logged, nothing raised, no metric moved. The system had no way to tell an operator that its\n   primary dedup key had never been populated.\n2. **A working fallback hid it.** The SECONDARY body-equality skip — described in-code as\n   belt-and-suspenders for \"a lost map write\" — absorbed enough traffic that the failure looked\n   like partial success rather than total failure of the primary path. Short comments deduped\n   (REB-424: 22/25); only richly-formatted ones did not (REB-1861: 1/40). A degradation that\n   correlates with content shape is exactly the kind that reads as flakiness.\n3. **A prior fix in the same area did not surface it.** `florid-vigorous-urial`\n   (`1f3d-cb66-d3fc-4356`) fixed 5000-cap comment inflation via pagination, and that fix is\n   present and correct. It **hardened the fallback's inputs while the primary skip was already\n   inert** — so the area had recent, competent attention that did not reveal the deeper problem.\n   That is the most interesting fact here.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis is an **escape analysis**, in the manner of `cranial-goodly-seahog`. It should produce\nfindings and, where warranted, concrete detection proposals — NOT a re-fix of the bridge, which\n`aa7b` owns.\n\nQuestions to answer with evidence:\n\n- **Detection.** What signal existed that a reasonable operator or test could have read? Was\n  `comment_ids` ever non-empty — i.e. did this regress, or was it never populated? Git history on\n  `.bridge_state/bindings.json` should settle that, and the answer changes the analysis\n  completely.\n- **Test gap.** Do the reconciler's tests exercise the primary ID skip at all, or do they pass a\n  binding store that makes the fallback sufficient? A test that would still pass with\n  `is_comment_mapped` hard-wired to `False` cannot detect this class.\n- **Review gap.** Would any current plan-review or code-review criterion have flagged a\n  silently-returning guard on a correctness-critical path? If not, is that a criterion worth\n  proposing — bearing in mind the project's rule that a capability whose only trigger is a\n  specific CI system is not portable (`project.portability`).\n- **Observability gap.** Should a dedup map that is empty while its sibling map holds thousands\n  of entries be an alertable invariant? This is the cheapest candidate remedy and should be\n  evaluated on its merits rather than assumed.\n- **Fallback design.** The general pattern — a secondary guard silently carrying a failed\n  primary — is likely not unique to this file. Is it worth a targeted sweep?\n\n## Testing / Verification\n\nThe deliverable is an analysis, so verification is reproducibility of each finding: the\nfindings comment must quote the exact probe commands run and their raw output — the\n`git log`/`git show` commands over the `tickets` branch history of\n`.bridge_state/bindings.json` (populated-or-not per revision), the pytest invocation that\ndemonstrates whether the named dedup tests pass with `is_comment_mapped` forced to `False`\n(run in this worktree's `.venv`), and the `rebar explain <criterion-id>` /\n`docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` reads behind the review-gap conclusion. Any reader can\nre-run those commands and reproduce each classification.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] [non-codebase] Established from evidence (git history of `.bridge_state/bindings.json`)\n      whether `comment_ids` was ever populated, so the escape is correctly classified as a\n      regression versus never-worked — checked by reading the findings comment's quoted\n      history probe.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The test gap is characterised: named tests that cover the dedup path, and\n      a demonstration of whether they would still pass with the primary skip disabled —\n      checked by the quoted pytest run in the findings comment.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The review gap is characterised: whether any existing plan-review or\n      code-review criterion addresses silently-inert guards, with the criterion ids checked —\n      checked by the criterion-registry citations in the findings comment.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] At least one concrete, portable detection proposal is made — or a\n      reasoned argument that none is worth its cost, which is an acceptable outcome if argued\n      — checked by reading the findings comment; any gate/criterion change is filed for\n      operator adjudication, not implemented.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] Findings are recorded on this ticket; any proposed criterion or test work\n      is filed as its own ticket linked `discovered_from` this one rather than implemented\n      here — checked by the links on this ticket.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nWas blocked by `aa7b-5e47-6d3d-4615` (now closed): its root-cause pass confirmed the mechanism —\nGUARD 3 of `_record_comment_id` (`dispatch_apply_phases.py:328-330`) fires on every successful\nCloud post because ACLI's batch mutation envelope carries no top-level `id`; guards 1/2/4 and\nthe persistence path are healthy. That confirmed mechanism is the input this analysis builds on."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787395770312965001,"uuid":"f3e57ef3-a555-41a4-81e2-39259214e0de"}